  HEADLINE, RADIUS-MATCHED (the only defensible comparison):
    X-COP median R500        requirement exceeds measurement by 1.69x - 6.78x (1.14x - 4.55x at +1 sigma)
    X-COP median R200        requirement exceeds measurement by 0.95x - 3.81x (0.68x - 2.70x at +1 sigma)
    Coma <R500 incl. bulk    requirement exceeds measurement by 1.00x - 4.00x (0.56x - 2.22x at +1 sigma)
    The naive core factor (3.8x - 15.4x against A2029's 2.6%) is RADIUS-MISMATCHED and
    must NOT be the headline: every XRISM velocity measurement stops inside 1 Mpc,
    exactly where KL themselves say the bias 'should play a minor role'.

  AND THE SIGN, WHICH IS THE HARDER HALF.  Two distinct statements, both unfavourable:
    (a) AT r>1 Mpc the requirement's SIGN is right (true mass ABOVE thermal-HSE mass) but
        the magnitude is 1.7x-6.8x short of the radius-matched measurement.
    (b) AT R500 AND INWARD the residual is a DEFICIT (MOND under-predicts).  Erasing
        eta by a mass-bias route needs the thermal HSE to OVER-estimate the truth by
        1 - 1/eta = 57.1% (median eta=2.334) to 60.7% (geomean eta=2.542); on the alt
        footing 53.1% to 56.9%.  Non-thermal pressure does the OPPOSITE: M_true =
        M_HSE/(1-f_nth) >= M_HSE for f_nth >= 0, so it RAISES eta.  The turbulence escape
        at R500 is not merely too small -- it has the WRONG SIGN.  With the measured
        f_nth = 5.9% at R500 the canonical eta moves from 2.542 to 2.701.
    (b') *** CATEGORY CAVEAT, added 2026-07-30 after an adversarial check. *** The eta above is
        built on eRASS1 M500, which is a WEAK-LENSING-CALIBRATED X-ray count-rate proxy, NOT a
        thermal-HSE mass. So the M_true = M_HSE/(1-f_nth) argument does not apply to it
        directly, and quoting it here as though it did overstates the case against the
        turbulence escape. The relevant systematic for a WL-calibrated mass is the WL
        CALIBRATION (Li+2024: WL runs ~110%% above hydrostatic AND kinematic, which agree with
        each other) -- which is exactly the axis the corpus previously mislabelled as
        'post-XRISM'. The f_nth arithmetic below is retained as a SIGN argument for genuinely
        hydrostatic analyses (e.g. Kelleher & Lelli's), not as a correction to this eta.
        [both footings: alt eta 2.321 -> 2.466]

  LEGITIMATE SOFTENERS -- the tightening is real but NOT clean.  All five are sourced:
    (i)   RADIUS.  A2029 stops at R2500 = 670 kpc; Perseus at ~0.7 R2500 ~ 570 kpc.  Neither
          directly bounds a requirement stated at r > 1 Mpc.
    (ii)  LINE-OF-SIGHT / FIELD OF VIEW.  The 2.6-3.1% figures are small-scale LOS
          broadening and explicitly exclude bulk-velocity pressure (ApJL 985 L20 Sect 4.1).
          Folding bulk motions in, Coma reaches P_NT/P_tot(<R500) = 10 (+8/-4)% (A&A 704 A35
          Eq 16) -- which sits ON KL's lower bound, so at R200 the ranges OVERLAP and there
          is no exclusion.
    (iii) SCATTER.  X-COP's per-cluster range at R500 is 2.2% to 43.6%; the 5.9% is a
          MEDIAN, and Eckert's own bound on the population MEAN is 13%, not 6%.  MOND
          cluster fits are done cluster-by-cluster, so individual clusters DO reach it.
    (iv)  MODEL DEPENDENCE OF THE ONE RADIUS-MATCHED NUMBER.  X-COP's alpha at R500 is not
          kinematic: it is inferred from f_gas,HSE against a LambdaCDM-calibrated universal
          gas fraction (Omega_b/Omega_m = 0.156, Planck; Y_b from The300).  Using it against
          MOND is partly circular.  XRISM's numbers are model-independent but core-only --
          the two virtues do not coexist in one dataset.
    (v)   NOT AN IDENTITY.  KL's 'bias' is a mass/acceleration residual, not P_NT/P_tot.
          The empirical (mass bias)/alpha ratio from Eckert Table 2 has median 1.10 at R500
          and 0.86 at R200, so the factors above carry ~30% definitional slop.
    NOT a valid softener: f_nth is algebraically IDENTICAL across all four XRISM/X-COP
    papers (all credit Eckert+2019 Eq 10), so definitional mismatch cannot be invoked there.

  ON THE FRAMEWORK'S OWN FOOTINGS the comparison gets slightly WORSE, not better: KL used
  a0 = 1.2e-10, and in the deep limit the framework predicts less acceleration --
    canonical rho_DE  (cH_Lambda/Z)    sqrt(a0/1.2e-10) = 0.8832 -> 11.7% LESS deep-MOND acceleration than KL
    alt rho_total     (cH_0/Z)         sqrt(a0/1.2e-10) = 0.9709 -> 2.9% LESS deep-MOND acceleration than KL
  which pushes the required bias UP.  Direction only -- this is not a refit, and the
  framework's nu is not KL's, so no revised bias percentage is quoted from it.

